# nr-exthost-imaging-core: new crate mixdog-imaging (native/core/crates/mixdog-imaging), written; not yet a workspace member.
# Written without compiling (no cargo run in this wave); the first compile is the lead's. Every name and signature of the
# external crates it uses (image 0.25.9, moxcms 0.7.11, zune-jpeg 0.5.12, zune-core 0.5.1, sha2, base64) was read in the
# local registry sources, and the crate type-checks and passes clippy -D warnings against stubs of exactly those signatures.

## 1. native/core/Cargo.toml, [workspace] members: add the line (after "crates/mixdog-js")
    "crates/mixdog-imaging",

## 2. native/core/Cargo.toml, [workspace.dependencies]
Internal crates block, after the `mixdog-js = ...` line:
    mixdog-imaging = { path = "crates/mixdog-imaging" }
The `image` line (today `image = { version = "0.25", default-features = false, features = ["jpeg"] } # s3-04: ...`) becomes
    image = { version = "0.25", default-features = false, features = ["jpeg", "png", "gif", "tiff", "webp"] } # s3-04: JPEG encoder for the set-of-marks burn-in; nr-exthost-imaging-core: the decoders and the PNG/GIF/TIFF encoders of mixdog-imaging (the JPEG encoder of mixdog-imaging is its own, 4:2:0 + optimised Huffman like sharp)
External block (next to `image`):
    moxcms = "0.7.11"                    # mixdog-imaging: ICC and CMYK to sRGB (image 0.25.9 depends on it already)
    zune-core = { version = "0.5.1", default-features = false }   # mixdog-imaging: ZCursor, DecoderOptions, ColorSpace (image enables it with the same features)
    zune-jpeg = "0.5.12"                 # mixdog-imaging: the JPEG decoder in strict mode (a truncated file is an error, as for sharp), CMYK output, EXIF and ICC
## 3. Lock and hack
`cargo update --workspace --offline`: adds the package `mixdog-imaging` and, through the new `image` features, the crates image 0.25.9
already declares and the local cache has: png 0.18.1 (crc32fast, fdeflate, flate2 1.1.10, bitflags), gif 0.14.1 (color_quant 1.1.0,
weezl 0.1.12), tiff 0.10.3 (fax 0.2.6, half 2.7.1, quick-error, weezl, zstd 0.13.x with zstd-sys, zune-jpeg 0.4.21), image-webp 0.2.4
(byteorder-lite). Nothing outside the cache is added. mixdog-imaging's own edges: base64, image, moxcms, sha2, zune-core, zune-jpeg,
mixdog-workspace-hack; dev: mixdog-testkit, serde_json. Then `cargo hakari generate` (the new `image` features, and flate2/zstd
features they pull, are unified into mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml).

## 4. Dependency edges of later tasks (not applied here)
mixdog-exthost-worker (nr-exthost-worker-methods) takes `mixdog-imaging = { workspace = true }` and answers image.probe / image.jpeg /
image.validate / image.resize from it:
    image.probe    -> mixdog_imaging::probe(&bytes)                                  ok {width, height} (both nonzero), Err -> invalid or environment by err.class()
    image.jpeg     -> mixdog_imaging::encode_tool_image_jpeg(&bytes, w, h, allowEnlargement, quality)   ok {data, width, height}
    image.validate -> mixdog_imaging::validate(&bytes, format)  (the param is `format`, not `ext`); an empty buffer is Err(environment), as Node answers
    image.resize   -> mixdog_imaging::resize_image_buffer(&bytes, ext, ResizeOptions { profile, ..default })
                      Ok(None) for an empty buffer, Err(e) is {error:'invalid', message: e.message()} whatever its class (Node's resize handler
                      reports every INVALID_IMAGE_DATA that way); the composer's profile is mixdog_imaging::Profile::from_name / image_profile_for_provider.
image.still stays with nr-exthost-imaging-still (`still.rs` answers "lossy WebP encoding is not available" until then).
Until that encoder lands a resize that must WRITE WebP (a WebP input over the box or under the 200 px floor) answers an environment
failure naming it: the still task must land before the wiring flips image.resize and image.validate off the Node worker.
SERVICE_PENDING (compose_flip.rs): nothing to remove here; no protocol method is added by this crate.

## 5. Fixture set index: native/core/fixtures/MANIFEST.json `sets`, between "idle-sweep" and "inheritance-fit" (alphabetical)
    {
      "command": "node benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/record-image-codec.mjs --tree <tree of the Node product>",
      "fileCount": 99,
      "manifest": "image-codec/MANIFEST.json",
      "manifestSha256": "9da82ad5bc9e6cdc93a1a80beaa809ddb481b1a4ff0798d30e109c94c1e0bc3f",
      "recorder": "benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/record-image-codec.mjs",
      "set": "image-codec",
      "totalBytes": 18060781
    }
(verify-manifests.mjs recomputes these; re-run the recorder with --tree C:\Project\mixdog if the recorder's lib files changed, then refresh the row.)

## 6. Verify (after 1-3)
node benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/record-image-codec.mjs --tree C:\Project\mixdog
cargo nextest run -p mixdog-imaging --offline
cargo clippy -p mixdog-imaging --all-targets --offline -- -D warnings
cargo tree -p mixdog-imaging --offline          (resolves with no download)
The twin test (tests/node_twin.rs) is the one that can fail for a reason only a compile shows: pixel closeness of the decoders against libvips
(PSNR >= 38 dB, mean difference <= 2.0 on a 32x32 thumbnail) and a JPEG size within 15 percent. A failing case names itself; the lists in the
test (`PIXEL_APPROXIMATE`, `WEBP_ENCODER_PENDING`) say what is not compared and why.
